  "headline": "Sun, Sand and Portuguese Heritage",
  "short_description": "India's smallest state is famed for its beaches, Portuguese colonial architecture and laid-back coastal culture.",
  "about": "A former Portuguese colony until 1961, Goa blends Indian and Iberian influences across its churches, cuisine and beach towns stretching along the Arabian Sea.\n\nA coastal state on India's western Konkan coast, bordered by the Arabian Sea, Maharashtra and Karnataka.\n\nA fusion of Konkani and Portuguese influences visible in music, festivals (like Carnival) and cuisine.\n\nA mix of Hindu and Christian communities, with landmark churches and temples across the state.",
  "history": "Ruled by the Portuguese for over 450 years, giving Goa a distinct colonial architectural and culinary legacy unlike the rest of India.",
  "history_verified": false,
  "transportation": "- Goa International Airport (airport)\n- Manohar International Airport (airport)\n- Madgaon Railway Station (railway)\n- Kadamba Bus Stand, Panaji (bus terminal)",
  "travel_info": "Languages: Konkani, Marathi, English, Hindi\nBudget: premium",
  "best_time_text": "November to February",
  "ideal_duration_text": "3–5 days",
  "nearest_airport": "Goa International Airport, Dabolim (GOI) / Manohar International Airport (GOX)",
  "nearest_railway_station": "Madgaon (Margao) Railway Station",
